Detecting vulnerabilities in Windows kernel-mode device drivers is essential due to their privileged access, making them attractive targets for attackers. Static analysis methods leveraging symbolic execution and taint analysis have proven effective but they are constrained by severe path explosion and condition-sensitive branching. These limitations impede coverage and often prevent accurate identification of IOCTL dispatchers under loop-based or indirect registration patterns, inflating analysis cost and causing downstream vulnerability reasoning to miss key execution paths. We present SysDiver, a flow-aware static analysis framework that replaces symbolic execution with a lightweight, coarse-grained Reaching Definitions Analysis (RDA). SysDiver recovers I/O request—driven execution flows and integrates this flow context into a scalable taint engine, while employing a hybrid dispatcher identification strategy that combines pattern matching with localized RDA verification. SysDiver detects major vulnerability classes—including unsafe memory operations, arbitrary access, and security-sensitive API misuse—even in optimized or stripped binaries. In an evaluation on production drivers, SysDiver achieved high detection coverage, uncovered 45 previously unknown vulnerabilities, and required significantly less time and memory than state-of-the-art static driver analysis tools.